            <ArticleTitle>Adenomyosis in the adolescent population: a case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e146-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2009.01.067</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This report presents a rare cause of dysmenorrhea in the adolescent female and the usefulness of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with T2 weighted sequence in the identification of adenomyosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two adolescents who presented with persistent pelvic pain in the absence of outflow tract obstruction or biopsy confirmed endometriosis had MRI-findings consistent with adenomyosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the adolescent female presenting with persistent dysmenorrhea, a diagnosis of adenomyosis is rare but should remain a possibility as one considers the differential diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
